#limitation(title: [NMES Safety Not Established in ME/CFS — NOT a Clinical Recommendation])[
NMES is presented here as a *hypothetical* countermeasure only. No ME/CFS-specific NMES safety or efficacy trial exists. The critical unanswered question is whether electrically-induced muscle contractions trigger PEM: if the metabolic cost of muscle activation is the PEM trigger, NMES contractions — which are metabolically indistinguishable from voluntary contractions — could trigger PEM identically to volitional exercise. This is not a merely theoretical concern: hybrid electrical stimulation raises oxygen uptake and heart rate comparably to voluntary effort @Tajima2021hybridNMESoxygen, and electrical pulse stimulation of ME/CFS muscle fails to activate AMPK and glucose uptake @Brown2015EPSAMPK — a contraction-metabolic defect consistent with a PEM-prone response. A rare rhabdomyolysis case in electrical muscle stimulation documents a real, if uncommon, harm @vonDeneen2024EMSrhabdo. The counterweight is that the 1990s studies found normal electrically-stimulated force in ME/CFS @Gibson1993CFSmuscle @Edwards1993muscleHistopathology @Jamal1991postviralNeurophysiology — consistent with a central (not peripheral) limitation, in which case EMS might bypass the deficit and be relatively benign; the tension between this legacy null and the modern membrane/metabolic defects is unresolved and is exactly why EMS safety cannot be assumed in either direction without a trial. Ch17 @oq:bedbound-nmes articulates this: whether even 5 minutes at the lowest visible contraction intensity in a single muscle group is tolerated is completely unknown. The protocol below reflects ICU rehabilitation parameters @Dirks2015NMES (n=6, within-subject crossover, 7 days, fully sedated patients); ME/CFS-appropriate parameters, if any exist, would need to be far lower. Contraindicated in patients with pacemakers or implanted electrical devices.
]
